{
  "id": "numbers_station_broadcast",
  "name": "Numbers-station shortwave broadcast",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Cold War-era shortwave espionage broadcasts, active from the 1950s onward and still transmitting (Cuba's HM01 continues today): flat monotone read or machine-generated numeral groups sent to field agents for one-time-pad decryption. Stations announce themselves with repeating interval signals such as the 'Lincolnshire Poacher' folk-tune ident and the 'Swedish Rhapsody' music box, then recite number groups at a fixed metronomic pace before signing off. The aesthetic — affectless voice buried in shortwave carrier hiss, heterodyne whistles, and fading — was canonized by The Conet Project (Irdial-Discs, 1997), a four-CD anthology of off-air recordings that made the genre a touchstone for experimental musicians. Operating agencies almost never acknowledge the stations, and their purpose is known largely through espionage trials and defector accounts.",
  "recipe": "classic North African mid-1950s numbers-station shortwave broadcast, spoken Word, broadcast branch audiobook narration documentary voiceover. thick, breath veiled tone, slow rate, interlocked note trading. cement block broadcast booth. dynamic moving-coil, transformer-coupled solid-state pre, dbx 160 VCA, parametric EQ. cassette.",
